Z||000008||Dave Gunsul||08-15-2001||12:18 PM||||I just got an email from Performance distributors. They do indeed make a noncomputer controlled HEI dist. for the 60 deg. V6,Part# 16720.<BR>The price is a little steep at $289.00  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> but it comes with a custom advance curve made just for your particular engine mods, a high dwell module, and a 50,000 volt coil. <BR>Performance Distributors ph# is (901)-396-5782. 2699 Barris DR. Memphis, TN. 38132.<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000009||2M4 Dale||08-15-2001||11:39 PM||||I am definatlly thinking about doing this......Hey isthiswhereiputausername......how does your carbureted V6 perform compared to the injection ??? How is your throttle response ? Fuel ecomony ??? drivability ??? etc.||||reg||1
Z||000010||isthiswhereiputausername?||08-16-2001||12:30 AM||||What I did was use a 85 buick skylark motor I had.(parts for these motors were plentiful and cheap)<BR>Compared to a MPFI, performance is not as great,but its sure is good for a daily driver.<BR>(I'm sure you could equal or beat a stock mpfi if you do the addons you menioned) <P><BR>I find it VERY dependable & easy to work on as far as motors go..no hassles from computers or anything.. and has pretty good gas mileage (depending on how I feel like driving hehe)<P>I (at the time) didnt have any V6 fiero mounts so I had to fabricate some(fixed now) <BR>I would suggest having the correct mounts(lot easier)   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>As far as hookup, It went in really easy, wiring wasnt too hard either, All my dash works,heat/oil/etc..<P>I'm using my stock 4cyl rad, It does not overheat at all.<P>I would definely do it again(Could'nt stand the Duke it had)<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by isthiswhereiputausername? (edited 08-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Rob Durham||08-16-2001||05:30 PM||||87 and newer engines will need to have the heads changed to the pre 87 heads in order for the stock manifold or any after market manifold to be used or you will have to have a custom manifold built<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://rs-designs.com/87GT.html" TARGET=_blank>  <IMG SRC="http://rs-designs.com/Banner.jpg">  </A>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Dave Gunsul||08-16-2001||08:04 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Rob Durham:<BR><B>87 and newer engines will need to have the heads changed to the pre 87 heads in order for the stock manifold or any after market manifold to be used or you will have to have a custom manifold built<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I think that you're thinking of the 4 cyl heads not the V6 heads. All the V6 heads are the same for the Fiero from 85 to 88.<BR>The 4cyl's changed their heads in 87.<P><P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000013||Rob Durham||08-16-2001||08:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Dave Gunsul:<BR><B> I think that you're thinking of the 4 cyl heads not the V6 heads. All the V6 heads are the same for the Fiero from 85 to 88.<BR>The 4cyl's changed their heads in 87.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Nope 2.8 V6 heads<P>You dont have to take my word for it, call GM, Edelbrock, or Weiand and check it out.<P>GM changed the head design in 87 and quit putting carbs on their engines!<P>||||reg||1
